<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=battering-ram">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Weapons and war</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battering ram</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as battering rams</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A battering ram is a siege weapon consisting of a heavy beam used to break down gates and walls. The Assyrian army developed wheeled, armoured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siege-engine">siege engines</a> with rams, shown on palace reliefs. Such siege technology allowed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyrian Empire</a> to capture fortified cities across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/near-east">Near East</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battering-ram.jpg" alt="Battering ram" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Battering_ram.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>A battering ram is a siege engine designed to break open the masonry walls of fortifications or to splinter their wooden gates. In its simplest form it is a large, heavy log carried by several people and driven against an obstacle. Later rams enclosed the log in an arrow-proof, fire-resistant canopy mounted on wheels, with the log swung inside from chains or ropes. Many canopies had curved or slanted wooden roofs covered in fresh wet hides, which reduced the risk of fire and protected the crew from arrows and javelins.</p><p>Rams worked because stone and brick were weak in tension and cracked under force. With repeated blows the cracks grew until a breach opened. A capped ram had an iron or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steel">steel</a> head, sometimes shaped like the head and horns of a sheep, which did more damage but weighed more to carry.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The earliest depiction of a possible ram is in the tomb of Khety, a noble of the 11th Dynasty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, where two soldiers advance on a fortress under a mobile roofed shelter, carrying a long pole. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> in the ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-east">Middle East</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> the log was slung from a wheeled frame. An Assyrian image of the 9th century BCE shows defenders trying to burn a ram with torches and to trap it with a chain, while the wet hides on the canopy protect it from the flames.</p><p>The first confirmed use in the Occident was in 503 to 502 BCE, when the Roman consul Opiter Verginius attacked Pometia, according to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livy">Livy</a>. The Spartans used rams at the siege of Plataea in 427 BCE, and rams with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siege-tower">siege towers</a> appeared in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> at the siege of Selinus in 409 BCE.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>Defenders answered rams by dropping obstacles such as a sack of sawdust in front of the head, by catching the log with grappling hooks, by setting the ram on fire, or by making a sudden sally. Some rams ran on rollers instead of hanging, so that they gained more speed, and Vitruvius describes one used by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a>.</p><p>Rams influenced the design of defensive walls, and they were used in sieges of major cities, among them the Roman destruction of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">Crusades</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sack-of-rome-410">Sack of Rome</a> in 410 and the sieges of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>. The siege <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannons</a> of the later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> ended their use. A story that the rhyme about Humpty Dumpty concerns a ram at the siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gloucester">Gloucester</a> in 1643 is almost certainly untrue, and began in a spoof essay of 1956.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battering ram” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Engineers used wheeled <b>battering rams</b>, siege towers, ramps and mining to reduce heavily fortified cities, as the reliefs of Sennacherib's siege of Lachish in 701 BCE record.</blockquote><a href="/empire/assyrian-empire">Assyrian Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Hittites conducted systematic siege warfare, with <b>battering rams</b> and siege works described in the Old Hittite account of the siege of Urshu, though their preferred method remained political isolation rather than costly direct assault.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hittite-empire">Hittite Empire · Military</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>9th century BCE</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>Assyria</dd></div><div><dt>Credited to</dt><dd><a href="/empire/assyrian-empire">Assyrian Empire</a></dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>A possible battering ram, carried by soldiers under a mobile roofed structure, is shown in the tomb of the 11th Dynasty Egyptian noble Khety; it is said only that the pole may represent a simple ram.</p><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=battering-ram">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
